{"title":"Count Arrays With Adjacent Difference at Most 1","difficulty":"Medium","statement":"You are given an array `arr` of length `n` and an integer `M`. Some entries of `arr` are `0`, meaning the value is missing; every other entry is already a positive integer between `1` and `M`.\n\nReplace every `0` with a positive integer from `[1, M]` (each zero is chosen independently). A completed array is **valid** if every pair of neighbouring elements differs by at most 1:\n\n`|arr[i] - arr[i + 1]| ≤ 1` for all `0 ≤ i < n - 1`.\n\nCount the number of valid completed arrays. Two completions are different if they differ in at least one position. Since the count can be huge, print it **modulo** `1000000007`.","inputFormat":"The first line contains two integers `n` and `M`.\nThe second line contains `n` integers `arr[0], ..., arr[n-1]`.","outputFormat":"Print a single integer: the number of valid completed arrays modulo 1000000007.","constraints":["1 ≤ n ≤ 1000","1 ≤ M ≤ 1000","0 ≤ arr[i] ≤ M"],"examples":[{"input":"3 3\n0 2 0","output":"9","explanation":"The middle value is fixed at 2. Each end may be 1, 2 or 3, because all three differ from 2 by at most 1, and the two ends are chosen independently: 3 × 3 = 9."},{"input":"4 3\n0 0 0 0","output":"41","explanation":"Let f[v] be the number of valid prefixes ending in value v. For length 1, f = [1, 1, 1]. Each next f[v] is the sum of f[v-1], f[v], f[v+1] (those in range), giving [2, 3, 2] for length 2, [5, 7, 5] for length 3 and [12, 17, 12] for length 4. The total is 12 + 17 + 12 = 41."},{"input":"3 4\n1 0 4","output":"0","explanation":"The middle value x would need |1 - x| ≤ 1 and |x - 4| ≤ 1, which means x ≤ 2 and x ≥ 3. No value works, so there are no valid arrays."}],"tests":[{"input":"1 1\n0","output":"1"},{"input":"1 1\n1","output":"1"},{"input":"1 5\n0","output":"5"},{"input":"1 3\n3","output":"1"},{"input":"4 2\n1 0 0 2","output":"4"},{"input":"5 4\n4 0 0 0 1","output":"4"},{"input":"3 3\n2 2 2","output":"1"},{"input